WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.260
StyleMorph is a 3D-aware generative model that
pulls apart the four things that make an

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.945
image what it is: the object's 3D shape,

00:00:09.025 --> 00:00:13.040
the camera pose, the object's appearance, and the
background.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.544
It does this by learning a 3D morphable model from
nothing but ordinary 2D photos,

00:00:18.624 --> 00:00:23.042
with no 3D scans, no pose labels, and no template
supplied by hand.

00:00:23.122 --> 00:00:30.493
The trick is to morph a single canonical 3D
template and render its coordinates into a purely geometric 2D map,

00:00:30.573 --> 00:00:35.259
which then conditions a StyleGAN renderer for
photorealistic synthesis.

00:00:35.339 --> 00:00:37.876
On faces, cats, dogs, and wild animals,

00:00:37.956 --> 00:00:43.179
StyleMorph matches state-of-the-art image quality
while giving you independent,

00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:46.670
fine-grained control over every factor of variation.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:51.224
The dream in 3D-aware image generation is a model
you can steer,

00:00:51.304 --> 00:00:54.946
one where you change the pose without touching identity,

00:00:55.026 --> 00:00:58.469
or swap the background without disturbing the object.

00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:01.726
Today's best 3D-aware GANs make beautiful images,

00:01:01.806 --> 00:01:05.914
but their hybrid design fuses geometry and
appearance together,

00:01:05.994 --> 00:01:08.905
so editing one factor bleeds into the others.

00:01:08.985 --> 00:01:13.292
Classical 3D morphable models solve the control
problem elegantly,

00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:18.078
yet they require expensive 3D scanning and
painstaking manual alignment,

00:01:18.158 --> 00:01:21.202
which is why they mainly exist for human faces.

00:01:21.282 --> 00:01:27.251
StyleMorph asks whether we can get morphable-model
control for arbitrary object categories,

00:01:27.331 --> 00:01:30.840
learning everything from unstructured 2D images alone.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:37.984
Morphable models are the workhorse of visual
effects and augmented reality because they hand creators clean,

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:41.279
separate dials for pose, expression, and appearance.

00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:48.629
The question that motivates this work is simple:
can we get that same level of control inside a modern 3D-aware GAN,

00:01:48.709 --> 00:01:53.318
but without any of the 3D supervision that
morphable models normally need?

00:01:53.398 --> 00:01:59.592
Prior work that added 3D deformations to synthesis
either stayed limited to a single dynamic scene,

00:01:59.672 --> 00:02:04.218
or leaned on a deformable template that already
existed for the category.

00:02:04.298 --> 00:02:11.441
StyleMorph instead turns the morphable model itself
into something the network discovers from unlabeled 2D images,

00:02:11.521 --> 00:02:14.990
making it a first-class citizen of generative modelling.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:19.112
StyleMorph makes three linked contributions.

00:02:19.192 --> 00:02:22.933
First,

00:02:23.013 --> 00:02:26.755
it learns a 3D morphable model of an object
category's non-rigid shape variation using nothing but 2D images,

00:02:26.835 --> 00:02:30.576
by morphing a canonical template through backpropagation.

00:02:30.656 --> 00:02:34.398
Second, it introduces Template Object Coordinates,

00:02:34.478 --> 00:02:38.219
or TOCS: a deformable cousin of Normalized Object
Coordinates that gives every surface

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:42.041
point a stable template identity and acts as a powerful,

00:02:42.121 --> 00:02:45.862
deformation-equivariant descriptor of shape.

00:02:45.942 --> 00:02:49.684
Third,

00:02:49.764 --> 00:02:53.505
it feeds 2D TOCS maps as a purely geometric
conditioning signal into a StyleGAN-based deferred neural renderer,

00:02:53.585 --> 00:02:57.327
cleanly separating shape from appearance.

00:02:57.407 --> 00:03:01.148
Together these deliver disentangled control over
pose, shape, object appearance,

00:03:01.228 --> 00:03:04.970
and background, all at high resolution.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:06.971
The method has two halves.

00:03:07.051 --> 00:03:08.344
On the geometry side,

00:03:08.424 --> 00:03:14.035
a Morphable Renderer learns a deformation-free
object in canonical template coordinates

00:03:14.115 --> 00:03:16.128
as a constant implicit function.

00:03:16.208 --> 00:03:19.791
A SIREN-based deformation field, driven by a shape code,

00:03:19.871 --> 00:03:25.875
warps each camera ray from world space into that
template space by adding a predicted offset.

00:03:25.955 --> 00:03:31.631
Integrating the template coordinates along each ray
produces a 2D map called a TOCS map,

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:36.275
which encodes the compounded effects of shape
deformation, camera pose,

00:03:36.355 --> 00:03:39.284
and perspective, and nothing about appearance.

00:03:39.364 --> 00:03:40.723
On the synthesis side,

00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:46.348
a StyleGAN2-based deferred neural renderer takes
this geometric TOCS map together with

00:03:46.428 --> 00:03:48.507
two independent appearance codes,

00:03:48.587 --> 00:03:52.628
one for the foreground object and one for the
background scene,

00:03:52.708 --> 00:03:55.114
and generates a high-resolution image.

00:03:55.194 --> 00:04:00.804
The foreground and background are composited with
an alpha mask predicted by the model.

00:04:00.884 --> 00:04:06.430
Training proceeds in two stages: first the
deformable volume renderer learns realistic

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:10.158
shape at low resolution with weak silhouette supervision,

00:04:10.238 --> 00:04:15.914
then it is frozen and used only to supply TOCS maps
while the 2D renderer is trained for

00:04:15.994 --> 00:04:18.400
photorealistic full-resolution output.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:22.780
StyleMorph is evaluated on four widely used image
datasets.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:30.455
FFHQ contributes seventy thousand centered human
face photos with a range of challenging backgrounds and poses.

00:04:30.535 --> 00:04:36.401
The AFHQ collection adds animal faces in three
splits: roughly fifty-six hundred cats,

00:04:36.481 --> 00:04:40.481
fifty-two hundred dogs, and fifty-two hundred wild
animals.

00:04:40.561 --> 00:04:45.598
The authors report Frechet Inception Distance at
two-fifty-six resolution,

00:04:45.678 --> 00:04:50.092
comparing against eleven state-of-the-art 3D-aware
GAN baselines,

00:04:50.172 --> 00:04:56.039
so the results test both photorealism on faces and
the ability to generalize to animal

00:04:56.119 --> 00:04:59.980
categories that classical morphable models never covered.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:05.632
The headline finding is that disentanglement does
not have to cost image quality.

00:05:05.712 --> 00:05:11.315
StyleMorph reaches a Frechet Inception Distance of
seven point nine one on FFHQ faces,

00:05:11.395 --> 00:05:16.006
and four point two nine on cats, three point four
nine on wild animals,

00:05:16.086 --> 00:05:20.631
and thirteen point nine five on dogs, all at
two-fifty-six resolution.

00:05:20.711 --> 00:05:27.371
These numbers sit right alongside the strongest
3D-aware GANs that offer none of StyleMorph's control.

00:05:27.451 --> 00:05:31.270
The comparison that matters most is against
Disentangled3D,

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:37.151
the only prior method that also represents shape
with a deformable template: StyleMorph's

00:05:37.231 --> 00:05:42.701
FFHQ score of seven point nine one is far ahead of
its twenty-eight point one eight,

00:05:42.781 --> 00:05:46.996
and StyleMorph additionally separates foreground
from background,

00:05:47.076 --> 00:05:48.780
which that method does not.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.887
The ablations, all run under a fair ninety-six-hour
training budget,

00:05:52.967 --> 00:05:56.694
isolate each design choice.

00:05:56.774 --> 00:06:00.501
Swapping TOCS for plain NOCS raises FID from about
eight point three to eight point nine

00:06:00.581 --> 00:06:04.309
and blurs the conditioning signal,

00:06:04.389 --> 00:06:08.116
showing template-space coordinates matter.

00:06:08.196 --> 00:06:11.923
Removing the deformable module and using a direct
occupancy network instead keeps FID

00:06:12.003 --> 00:06:15.730
similar but drastically worsens the consistency scores,

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:19.537
proving the value of modelling shape through deformation.

00:06:19.617 --> 00:06:23.344
Switching from late fusion to early fusion nudges
FID down slightly but badly hurts alpha consistency,

00:06:23.424 --> 00:06:27.151
letting foreground and background bleed together.

00:06:27.231 --> 00:06:30.959
Finally,

00:06:31.039 --> 00:06:34.766
directly optimizing view consistency as a
differentiable loss does improve that metric,

00:06:34.846 --> 00:06:38.573
from nearly sixteen down to eight, but it trades
away image quality,

00:06:38.653 --> 00:06:42.380
pushing FID up to twelve point three.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:45.007
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:06:45.087 --> 00:06:49.167
On FFHQ faces StyleMorph scores a seven point nine
one FID,

00:06:49.247 --> 00:06:54.879
versus twenty-eight point one eight for the only
competing template-based method.

00:06:54.959 --> 00:07:00.590
On animals it reaches four point two nine on cats,
three point four nine on wild,

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.199
and thirteen point nine five on dogs.

00:07:03.279 --> 00:07:09.122
It disentangles four independent factors: shape,
camera pose, foreground appearance,

00:07:09.202 --> 00:07:10.956
and background appearance.

00:07:11.036 --> 00:07:17.302
And the core TOCS-versus-NOCS ablation shows
template coordinates improving FID from eight

00:07:17.382 --> 00:07:22.238
point nine to eight point three while also
sharpening disentanglement.

00:07:22.318 --> 00:07:28.020
All of this is learned from unstructured 2D images,
with no 3D supervision at all.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:36.099
The one-line takeaway is that StyleMorph delivers
morphable-model control inside a state-of-the-art image generator,

00:07:36.179 --> 00:07:38.849
learned entirely from ordinary 2D photos.

00:07:38.929 --> 00:07:44.952
By morphing a learned canonical template and
rendering it into a purely geometric TOCS map,

00:07:45.032 --> 00:07:49.646
it hands a StyleGAN renderer a clean geometric
signal, so shape, pose,

00:07:49.726 --> 00:07:50.853
object appearance,

00:07:50.933 --> 00:07:56.285
and background can each be edited independently
without sacrificing photorealism.

00:07:56.365 --> 00:08:01.985
In effect it builds an unsupervised 3D morphable
model for general object categories,

00:08:02.065 --> 00:08:08.558
extending the fine-grained controllability that
visual-effects artists rely on from faces to cats,

00:08:08.638 --> 00:08:10.100
dogs, and wild animals.
